ething that's often  unappreciated in taiwan context is if we  lose taiwan we will have to dramatically  compensate both in pure power terms  taiwan is a large economy it's got a  significant you know power position but  it's also people are really going to  question our credibility so we're going  to have to add a lot more countries to  our effort and that's going to create  new vulnerabilities that people don't  adequately appreciate if the united  states and its allies were to win a  conflict in taiwan you write about  increasing the burden of escalation  that is persuading china that a small  conflict over taiwan shouldn't become a  large-scale war  how do you persuade beijing fearing a  wider decline not to lash out  well i don't want to minimize what the  war over taiwan would be it would be a  large-scale war but i think it would not  necessarily become a total war which is  a possibility uh we can't be sure what  would happen uh but we'd have to be  prepared for a limited war because if  china's prepared for a limited war and  we're not we will always be checkmated  because they'll be willing to uh risk  take risks that we won't also be willing  to do uh the key there is not only to  the the burden of escalation is not only  about the costs that we can impose you  know so the fact that china will uh  induce uh the united states and others  to impose more costs on them it's also  about the china having uh sort of a  graceful exit a reason to acknowledge  if not defeat frustration i mean i think  the example i make and it's not a great  one but it's satisfactory is the  situation on the korean peninsula since  1953 there's no peace agreement it's  still an armistice but that's been  satisfactory for us and south korea uh  which is obviously a marvel of  development and so forth so i think what  we will need to think about in this  context actually is not only what are  the costs and penalties we can impose on  china but what are the reasons china  will have  t