ng them a  commitment that would be comparable to  the one we offer to our nato allies um  but again that downplays the associated  costs right it obligates us to go to war  if the chinese were to try something  against taiwan and you know it doesn't  even have to be sort of a full frontal  military assault right there are lots of  um you know what we used to call salami  slicing during the cold war there's lots  of things that china could do um to call  that commitment into question and if we  have made a concrete commitment um then  u.s policy makers find our options are a  lot more limited than they would be  otherwise  it does seem however with the creation  of also this coalition between australia  the uk and the u.s that there is more  appetite now uh certainly here in the uk  but but also in washington dc to be much  more upfront about the pushback that  china should expect to receive if it  were to step over certain red lines i  think that's true and to be clear just  because i'm arguing that we shouldn't  give a concrete security commitment to  taiwan doesn't mean that i don't think  that a rising china is somewhat of a  threat to the us  it doesn't mean that i'm not worried  about chinese advances in asia  but taiwan is a very small piece of that  overall puzzle there are major countries  in the region that the us has already  aligned with japan and south korea that  we're allied with  there are a variety of small diverse  countries in southeast asia you know  there's the whole indian subcontinent  that we can work with as you say the  indians the australians there's there  are lots of countries that the u.s can  work with that are more important than  taiwan um and so i'm not arguing that we  shouldn't be pushing back against china  i'm not arguing we should be concerned  about china i'm just saying i don't  think that taiwan  is the place that the us should be  making that as you say red line  emma lastly where do you think that this  power rivalry is he